    "message": "feat(llm): implement --format llm for the five commands that faked it\n\n`explain`, `read`, `status`, `doctor` and `savings` all advertised\n`text|json|llm` in their help and rendered `text` when given `llm`.\ndocs/llm-format.md documented the fallback as intentional — \"route to\nwhichever existing format is most compact\" — on the theory that prose and\nverbatim source have no record form.\n\nThat is true of the payload and false of everything around it. `explain`'s\nprose is a rendering of `facts` + `role` + `importance`, so an agent can have\nthe inputs instead and skip the paragraph entirely; `read`'s source was\narriving under a 4-column line-number gutter and a pair of ANSI escapes per\nline. `explain` is the one that mattered: it is the first call most plugins\nmake on any question, so the fallback was costing the most tokens on the\nhottest path.\n\nMeasured against `--format json`, which the help text sent agents to as the\nworkaround: explain -55%, doctor -31%, savings -31%, read -5%, and status\n+1%. `read` gains 23% against `text` instead, 601 bytes of which is gutter\nand ANSI on a 40-line read.\n\n`status` is kept despite being a wash on size: it is in for the explicit\n`stale=true|false` field, which is the question the command gets called to\nanswer and which `text` only implied through a warning line. Its unreachable\n-backend path now emits the spec's `error code=...` record so a consumer\nparses the failure the same way it parses a result.\n\nTwo exceptions stay on text and are now documented as such: `diff --content`\n(verbatim hunks) and `ingest`, a hidden implementation-detail command.\n\n`read`'s content block is the one place the one-record-per-line invariant is\nrelaxed. The preceding `content lines=<n>` record makes the block\nself-delimiting so a line of source can never be mistaken for a record.\n\nAlso corrects the CLAUDE.md paragraph on this, which was wrong in both\ndirections: it claimed `query` advertises `llm` (it offers only `text|json`)\nand missed `doctor`, `savings` and `ingest`. It also never mentioned that\nseven commands take no `--format` at all, where `--format llm` is an\n`unknown option` error rather than a fallback — the one case where an agent\nfollowing the \"pass it unconditionally\" advice breaks.",
